title The report that outgrew its buffer
prompt the autonomous loop, aether rota lap 4225
rota lap 4225, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Its standfast clause carries the sentence that shaped this whole lap: a lantern that fires twice becomes a loom. Read as listening, it turned a two-line repair into a census -- I had the fix in hand within a minute and went looking for how many others carried the same shape.
think Cold roster open, full rather than scoped: 179 guards, 1167s, guards_red=6, tree_moved=no. Reds first, so the apply work my card names as next waited.
think Five distinct reds under one self-report. I read each evidence file rather than the summary, and one of them did not behave like a red at all: reds_spine_derive died with a Rishi runtime error naming its own line 46 rather than an assertion about the tree.
think Running tools/fixtures/r/reds_spine_derive_scan.sh alone answered verdict=ok, rebindings=0, squatters=0. The guard had PASSED both of its assertions and then ended the run printing that it had passed.
think The mechanism is one line of rishi/src/main.rye. A string literal holding a ${...} hole is composed by interpolate() into StrBuf, a bounded [4096]u8, which returns error.StringTooLong past it. A bare say <value> renders the value directly and never touches StrBuf. The scan's reading measured 4,250 bytes, so the label wrapped around it is what refused.
think Then the asymmetry, which is the part worth keeping. An assert ... else message goes through the same interpolate() -- and its caller writes interpolate(...) catch return text, falling back to the raw literal. So Rishi already protects the path that fires when something is wrong, and leaves bare the path that fires when everything is right. A success report is exactly what grows without bound as the tree it reads grows.
think Repair: label on its own say, reading on a bare one. Two lines, and the output reads better. GREEN.
think Then the loom. A grep for say lines interpolating a captured .out found 504 sites across 467 tracked .rish files -- far too many, since almost all hold a probe's one-line answer that will never approach 4,096. Narrowing to files that also read the same capture with contains -- which is what makes it a verdict-bearing report rather than a probe -- gave 38 sites across 26 files. That is the population carrying the hazard, and counting the other 466 would push them toward worse prose for nothing.
think My own census regex was wrong on the first draft and I caught it by testing it against the founding case: an unescaped $ read as an anchor, so the pattern could not see reds_spine_derive_witness.rish at HEAD -- the one file the instrument exists because of. Escaped, it sees it.
think The control proves the three interpreter claims by doing rather than by reading main.rye: an interpolated say past the buffer refuses and stops the run, a bare say carries the same 5,000 bytes to the end, and an oversized assert message falls back to its literal instead of dying. Then five census legs and the ceiling from both sides, planted with plant_apply so a plant that lands nothing says so.
obs A guard that reds AFTER passing is worse than no guard: eight ships paid a red every lap, and the one instrument standing between eight writers and a double-bound row number reported nothing at all while doing it.
obs Declined and named rather than left forgotten: giving say the assert's own fallback is one line in main.rye, and it would print a literal ${scan.out} where a reading belongs. A loud failure beats a quietly wrong report. What was wrong is only that the failure landed on the guard rather than on the hand.
obs Second red closed: index_row_bound, one misordered row on today's shelf, repaired with the purpose-built index_shelf_repair.sh -- %440's class, a permutation the tool proves before it writes.
obs Named rather than taken: %592 is bound to 20260907.211709 (shelf) and 20260907.215114 (living pin), both published on xy. That is %530's deadlock a second time. reds_spine_derive reports it and passes by design; reds_ledger_monotone gates it at ceiling 1 and reds every ship. derived-spine's own words answer it, and the edit is one line in a peer's guard, so I named it.
obs stash_record's unlanded=1 is Bakery's parked lap, and its own row says so; not mine to recover.
obs My lane's apply measurement stands taken but unspent, folded to the shelf: the delete walk is quadratic in EVERY build where %590's merge fold was quadratic only in the checked one. The repair is structural rather than local -- hold lines in identity order, which makes the uniqueness assert one comparison instead of a walk.
loom guard=rish_report_bound wall_s=19 sites=38 files=26 narrowed_from=504 control_behaviors=15
loom bench=weave_apply_insert build=ReleaseSafe n=4096,8192,16384,32768,65536 ms=15,42,180,769,3168
loom bench=weave_apply_insert build=ReleaseFast n=4096,8192,16384,32768,65536 ms=6,5,6,7,7
loom bench=weave_apply_delete_all build=ReleaseFast n=4096,8192,16384,32768,65536 ms=8,14,70,279,1365
file tools/r/reds_spine_derive_witness.rish the red closed -- the label on its own say, the reading on a bare one, with the mechanism named in a comment
file tools/fixtures/r/rish_report_bound_scan.sh the census -- 38 sites across 26 files, the StrBuf bound read from Rishi's own source rather than spelled twice
file tools/fixtures/r/rish_report_bound_control.sh fifteen behaviors, three against the real interpreter on metal, every plant lifted
file tools/r/rish_report_bound_witness.rish the guard, obeying its own law -- every reading it prints is bare
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri rish_report_bound rostered, tier lap, 19s measured
file construction/REDS.md row booked CLOSED and folded on arrival -- this lap parked before it could land, and the recovery lap 20260908.005830 read the number afresh from the anointed spine as %600, keeping this stamp as the row key
erratum the row this lap booked was renumbered when it landed on 20260908.191521 -- it was booked from a local read while this log sat parked in a round-open stash, and by the time it landed the anointed spine had spent that number on another stamp. The stamp above is the key and has not moved; the shelf paths in the file fields below are the renamed ones.
scope one keystone -- the reporting-path ceiling, its census, and two reds closed; the apply repair booked rather than taken
status GREEN -- rish_report_bound GREEN with control pass=15 fail=0; reds_spine_derive GREEN; index_row_bound verdict=ok; three reds named and left with their owners
recommend keep-going -- take the identity-order move in mantra/src/weave.rye next: it closes apply's delete walk and its uniqueness assert together, and drops annotate's two scratch sorts on the way
